import{c as o,$ as i,Q as s,j as n,g as e,o as r,n as l,m as c}from"./chunks/framework.Ce_49mA8.js";const b=JSON.parse('{"title":"Finished Goods (Showcase)","description":"Complete guide to finished products inventory, FIFO batch tracking, dual cost pricing, stock intake, and inventory adjustments in OmniCraft ERP.","frontmatter":{"title":"Finished Goods (Showcase)","description":"Complete guide to finished products inventory, FIFO batch tracking, dual cost pricing, stock intake, and inventory adjustments in OmniCraft ERP.","datePublished":"2026-11-15","dateModified":"2026-11-25","order":8},"headers":[],"relativePath":"docs/spaces/production/showcase.md","filePath":"docs/spaces/production/showcase.md","lastUpdated":1795564800000}'),d={name:"docs/spaces/production/showcase.md"},h={id:"finished-goods-showroom-inventory-and-fifo-batches",tabindex:"-1"};function u(p,t,g,m,f,y){const a=i("DocIcon");return s(),n("div",null,[e("h1",h,[r(a,{name:"showcase"}),t[0]||(t[0]=l(" Finished Goods: Showroom Inventory and FIFO Batches ",-1)),t[1]||(t[1]=e("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#finished-goods-showroom-inventory-and-fifo-batches","aria-label":'Permalink to "Finished Goods: Showroom Inventory and FIFO Batches"'},"​",-1))]),t[2]||(t[2]=c('<p>In bespoke craftsmanship and small-batch production, finished goods sitting on the showroom shelf represent materialized working capital. Until an item is delivered into the customer&#39;s hands, every seam, polished edge, and coat of protective oil ties up real money: the purchase cost of consumed materials, the value of the craftsman&#39;s labor hours, and an allocated share of workshop overhead.</p><p>When showroom inventory is managed haphazardly or tracked in a generic spreadsheet, the maker inevitably loses financial clarity: older stock is sold at contemporary inflated rates, batch cost integrity collapses, and the storefront misleads buyers with out-of-date stock levels.</p><p>The <strong>&quot;Finished Goods&quot;</strong> tab in <strong>OmniCraft ERP</strong> serves as the central showroom inventory registry. It automatically receives products completed on the Workbench, tracks actual on-hand stock, preserves frozen FIFO cost batches, and computes recommended retail prices for sale.</p><hr><h2 id="display-modes-and-warehouse-navigation" tabindex="-1">Display Modes and Warehouse Navigation <a class="header-anchor" href="#display-modes-and-warehouse-navigation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Display Modes and Warehouse Navigation&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The finished goods interface is engineered for instant stock assessment and rapid inventory reconciliation. The top toolbar provides streamlined workspace controls:</p><ul class="craft-feature-list"><li><span class="craft-list-bullet"><svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"></polyline></svg></span><div><strong>Warehouse Rows (Default layout):</strong> a compact operational table featuring square 1:1 thumbnails, exact shelf quantities, actual weighted-average batch cost, calculated retail price, and quick action buttons for every item. Engineered for high-volume inventory management.</div></li><li><span class="craft-list-bullet"><svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"></polyline></svg></span><div><strong>Product Cards (Grid layout):</strong> a spacious visual showroom gallery featuring large photographs and detailed parameters. Ideal for periodic visual stock audits, catalog presentations to clients, and verifying public storefront formatting.</div></li></ul><h3 id="warehouse-filtering-and-instant-search" tabindex="-1">Warehouse Filtering and Instant Search <a class="header-anchor" href="#warehouse-filtering-and-instant-search" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Warehouse Filtering and Instant Search&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The central toolbar features a three-way status selector:</p><ul><li><strong>&quot;All&quot;:</strong> displays the complete roster of active workshop designs, including items with zero shelf stock (allowing you to quickly launch production or edit recipes).</li><li><strong>&quot;In Stock&quot;:</strong> isolates items that are physically present in the workshop right now (<code>stock &gt; 0</code>).</li><li><strong>&quot;Archived&quot;:</strong> reveals discontinued models or seasonal items temporarily withdrawn from active circulation.</li></ul><p>The search input in the right corner includes an instant clear button and filters items dynamically by name without reloading the view.</p><hr><h2 id="anatomy-of-an-inventory-product-row" tabindex="-1">Anatomy of an Inventory Product Row <a class="header-anchor" href="#anatomy-of-an-inventory-product-row" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Anatomy of an Inventory Product Row&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every row in the finished goods registry contains a comprehensive set of manufacturing, quantitative, and financial indicators:</p><ul><li><strong>Product Thumbnail (1:1):</strong> a high-resolution square preview automatically cropped from the bill of materials recipe or web showcase photos.</li><li><strong>Model Name:</strong> the full product title with quick access to viewing and editing.</li><li><strong>Stock Badge:</strong> a color-coded status chip. When stock is available, a green indicator appears (e.g., <em>&quot;In Stock: 4 pcs&quot;</em>); at zero stock, a neutral gray chip reads <em>&quot;Out of Stock&quot;</em>.</li><li><strong>Lifetime Manufacturing Counter:</strong> the <em>&quot;Total Manufactured: [X]&quot;</em> metric, recording the total volume of units crafted since the inception of the workshop database.</li><li><strong>Special Status Chips:</strong><ul><li><strong>&quot;Archived&quot;</strong> — the linked recipe has been moved to the archive;</li><li><strong>&quot;No Recipe&quot;</strong> — the item was added directly to stock without a preconfigured manufacturing recipe.</li></ul></li></ul><p>The right side of the row houses quick-action icon buttons: edit or create manufacturing recipe, open the inventory adjustment modal, archive the product, or restore it from the archive.</p><hr><h2 id="cost-accounting-mechanics-dual-cost-pricing-and-fifo-batches" tabindex="-1">Cost Accounting Mechanics: Dual Cost Pricing and FIFO Batches <a class="header-anchor" href="#cost-accounting-mechanics-dual-cost-pricing-and-fifo-batches" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cost Accounting Mechanics: Dual Cost Pricing and FIFO Batches&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A hallmark of professional workshop management is the clear separation between theoretical current manufacturing costs and the historical batch cost of existing inventory.</p><p>On each product row, OmniCraft ERP simultaneously computes and displays two independent cost figures:</p><ol><li><strong>Actual Stock Cost (Weighted Moving Average):</strong> the actual cost of the specific physical units currently sitting on your shelves, computed from the frozen batch costs of units completed on the Workbench.</li><li><strong>Calculated Recipe Cost (Theoretical Current):</strong> the projected cost to manufacture one unit &quot;right now,&quot; based on current raw material inventory prices and active workshop labor rates.</li></ol><div class="formula-hero-card"><div class="formula-card-top"><span class="formula-badge">Cost Accounting Mechanics</span><div class="formula-heading">Weighted Average Cost of On-Hand Inventory</div></div><div class="formula-equation-box"><div class="math-formula-display"><span class="math-var">On-Hand Stock Cost</span><span class="math-op">=</span><span class="math-var">Sum of Remaining Frozen Batch Costs</span><span class="math-op">÷</span><span class="math-var">Actual Physical Shelf Quantity</span></div></div></div><h3 id="first-in-first-out-fifo-in-real-workshop-practice" tabindex="-1">First-In, First-Out (FIFO) in Real Workshop Practice <a class="header-anchor" href="#first-in-first-out-fifo-in-real-workshop-practice" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;First-In, First-Out (FIFO) in Real Workshop Practice&quot;">​</a></h3><p>To relieve inventory during sales or write-offs, OmniCraft ERP applies the <strong>First-In, First-Out (FIFO)</strong> method. Inventory batches are depleted in the exact chronological order of their production.</p><div class="info custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Practical Example: Shielding Profit Margins from Material Inflation</p><p>Consider a real-world scenario in a leather workshop:</p><ol><li>One month ago, a craftsman produced three classic belts. Materials were inexpensive, freezing the actual cost of each belt at <code>$30</code>. The belts were placed on the shelf.</li><li>Today, the leather supplier increased prices by 40%. The Calculator indicates that producing the same belt right now costs <code>$45</code>.</li><li>A client enters the studio and purchases one belt.</li></ol><p>If the software automatically averaged costs across time or repriced existing belts using today&#39;s recipe rates, profit reporting would be distorted. OmniCraft ERP preserves batch history: the belt sold is deducted from the oldest batch at exactly <code>$30</code>. This guarantees clean management accounting and accurate net profit calculation.</p></div><hr><h2 id="recommended-retail-price-and-sales-channels" tabindex="-1">Recommended Retail Price and Sales Channels <a class="header-anchor" href="#recommended-retail-price-and-sales-channels" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Recommended Retail Price and Sales Channels&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Directly below the cost indicators, the system displays the calculated retail price. OmniCraft ERP establishes this figure via two synchronized mechanisms:</p><ul><li><strong>Automatic Markup Calculation:</strong> the cost price is multiplied by the designated markup percentage. Custom markup set within the product&#39;s bill of materials takes precedence. If no individual markup is defined, the global workshop markup from Space Settings (default <code>100%</code>) is applied.</li><li><strong>Fixed Price Priority from Sales Channels:</strong> if a fixed price is configured in the sales channels table (for instance, dedicated pricing for the &quot;Website&quot; channel or an external marketplace), the software surfaces that approved retail price instead.</li></ul><p>This safeguard protects the craftsman from accidentally selling items below operational profitability during sudden raw material price surges.</p><hr><h2 id="direct-stock-intake-bypassing-the-workbench" tabindex="-1">Direct Stock Intake (Bypassing the Workbench) <a class="header-anchor" href="#direct-stock-intake-bypassing-the-workbench" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Direct Stock Intake (Bypassing the Workbench)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In daily workshop operations, products do not always enter inventory strictly through the Workbench manufacturing queue. Certain workflows require bringing finished stock directly onto the books.</p><p>The left action toolbar features the <strong>&quot;Receive Goods&quot;</strong> button, opening a modal dialog for direct inventory intake.</p><ol class="craft-list-steps"><li><div><strong>Product Selection:</strong><br> Choose a catalog item or enter a name. If that name already exists in the catalog, the batch is added to the existing item. </div></li><li><div><strong>Intake Quantity:</strong><br> The number of finished units being added directly to warehouse inventory. </div></li><li><div><strong>Unit Cost Price:</strong><br> Lock in the unit cost for this independent FIFO inventory batch. </div></li><li><div><strong>Retail Price</strong><br> If you fill in “Retail Price (RRP, optional)”, the website channel price is saved with the batch. Use the storefront editor to control publication. </div></li></ol><div class="info custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Model for a stock item</p><p>In <strong>“Receive Finished Goods”</strong>, you can set <strong>“Product model”</strong> for a new or existing item without a recipe. Items with a recipe use the model selected in the Calculator.</p></div><h3 id="standard-intake-scenarios" tabindex="-1">Standard Intake Scenarios: <a class="header-anchor" href="#standard-intake-scenarios" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Standard Intake Scenarios:&quot;">​</a></h3><ol><li><strong>Entering Opening Balances:</strong> when onboarding onto OmniCraft ERP with pre-existing stock crafted prior to software adoption.</li><li><strong>Consignment and Partner Goods:</strong> taking in items crafted by partner artisans or collaborative studios for joint retail distribution.</li><li><strong>Showroom Sample Returns:</strong> returning display models from exhibitions, trade fairs, or photography studios if they were previously written off.</li></ol><div class="info custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Accounting Integrity: Zero Raw Material and Cash Impact</p><p>Direct finished goods intake registers a new incoming batch in showroom inventory, but <strong>does not consume raw materials from warehouse stock</strong> and <strong>generates no cash outflow entries in the Cash Flow Ledger</strong>. It is a pure asset recognition transaction.</p></div><hr><h2 id="inventory-adjustments-reconciling-discrepancies" tabindex="-1">Inventory Adjustments: Reconciling Discrepancies <a class="header-anchor" href="#inventory-adjustments-reconciling-discrepancies" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Inventory Adjustments: Reconciling Discrepancies&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Physical inventory occasionally diverges from software records: an item might sustain shelf damage, be misplaced, or surface during a seasonal studio cleanup. To reconcile these discrepancies, every product row and card features an <strong>&quot;Inventory Adjustment&quot;</strong> button.</p><p>The system supports two adjustment types:</p><ol><li><strong>&quot;Record Surplus (+)&quot;</strong>: increases physical shelf count. The &quot;Unit Cost&quot; field defaults to the current recipe cost from the Calculator, but the maker can manually override this figure. The software logs a new FIFO batch.</li><li><strong>“Write off loss (-)”</strong> deducts the specified quantity from the oldest batches first. If the shortage exceeds current stock, the adjustment is rejected and batch quantities are preserved.</li></ol><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Do Not Substitute Sales with Loss Write-Offs!</p><p>Writing off a loss removes the product without logging revenue. If an item was handed to a customer, gifted to a loyal patron, or discounted at 100%, <strong>always record it through the &quot;Sales&quot; tab</strong> with a <code>$0</code> retail price. This ensures the batch cost is accurately logged in Profit and Loss reports as a commercial marketing expense rather than an unaccounted warehouse shortage.</p></div><hr><h2 id="product-lifecycle-archiving-and-capital-protection" tabindex="-1">Product Lifecycle: Archiving and Capital Protection <a class="header-anchor" href="#product-lifecycle-archiving-and-capital-protection" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Product Lifecycle: Archiving and Capital Protection&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A workshop&#39;s product catalog evolves continuously: older designs are retired, and seasonal collections yield to new lines. OmniCraft ERP provides structured archiving alongside rigorous capital safeguards.</p><h3 id="archiving-a-product" tabindex="-1">Archiving a Product <a class="header-anchor" href="#archiving-a-product" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Archiving a Product&quot;">​</a></h3><p>To hide an item from the active inventory roster:</p><ol><li>Click the <strong>&quot;Archive product&quot;</strong> button (archive icon) on the right side of the item row.</li><li>Confirm the archive action. If a production order is open, complete the batch or cancel the order first.</li><li>Select <strong>“Archived”</strong> to find the item. Its stock and batches are preserved and can still be sold. Restore the item before starting a new production order.</li></ol><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Recipe Specifications Are Preserved</p><p>When a product is archived, its linked recipe in the Calculator automatically moves to the &quot;Archived Recipes&quot; folder. Raw material consumption ratios, labor time benchmarks, and overhead coefficients remain intact. You can restore the design to active production at any time by clicking the restore icon.</p></div><h3 id="restoring-and-deleting" tabindex="-1">Restoring and Deleting <a class="header-anchor" href="#restoring-and-deleting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Restoring and Deleting&quot;">​</a></h3><p>In the <strong>&quot;Archived&quot;</strong> filter view, two actions are available:</p><ul><li><strong>Restore Product (unarchive icon):</strong> returns the item to the active inventory catalog and re-enables its recipe on the Workbench.</li><li><strong>“Delete product”</strong> (trash icon) removes the item and linked recipe from the catalog. Completed transactions stay in the records.</li></ul><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Deletion requirements</p><p>You can delete a product when its stock balance is <code>0 pcs</code> and it has no open production orders. If stock remains, record a sale or use inventory adjustment to write off an actual shortage.</p></div><hr><h2 id="summary-matrix-of-warehouse-operations" tabindex="-1">Summary Matrix of Warehouse Operations <a class="header-anchor" href="#summary-matrix-of-warehouse-operations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Summary Matrix of Warehouse Operations&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Refer to this reference matrix to quickly verify the financial and inventory consequences of each operational action:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">Action in Software</th><th style="text-align:left;">Raw Materials Stock</th><th style="text-align:left;">Cash Flow Ledger</th><th style="text-align:left;">Finished Goods Batches</th><th style="text-align:left;">Workshop Capital Impact</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>Workbench Completion</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">Consumes raw materials</td><td style="text-align:left;">No change</td><td style="text-align:left;">Creates new FIFO batch</td><td style="text-align:left;">Value transfer: Raw Materials ➔ Finished Goods</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>Direct Stock Intake</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">No change</td><td style="text-align:left;">No change</td><td style="text-align:left;">Creates new FIFO batch</td><td style="text-align:left;">Increases inventory asset capital</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>Inventory: Surplus (+)</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">No change</td><td style="text-align:left;">No change</td><td style="text-align:left;">Creates new FIFO batch</td><td style="text-align:left;">Increases inventory asset capital</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>Inventory: Loss (-)</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">No change</td><td style="text-align:left;">No change</td><td style="text-align:left;">Depletes oldest FIFO batches</td><td style="text-align:left;">Decreases inventory equity (loss)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>Sale from Inventory</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">Consumes packaging stock</td><td style="text-align:left;">Inflow of revenue</td><td style="text-align:left;">Depletes oldest FIFO batches</td><td style="text-align:left;">Increases cash liquidity + gross profit</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>Move to Archive</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">No change</td><td style="text-align:left;">No change</td><td style="text-align:left;">Preserved intact</td><td style="text-align:left;">No change</td></tr></tbody></table><hr><h2 id="troubleshooting-common-pitfalls" tabindex="-1">Troubleshooting &amp; Common Pitfalls <a class="header-anchor" href="#troubleshooting-common-pitfalls" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Troubleshooting &amp; Common Pitfalls&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="faq-grid"><details class="faq-item"><summary class="faq-summary">What should I do if physical shelf counts do not match inventory figures in the software?</summary><div class="faq-content"><p>Open the inventory adjustment on the item card and enter the difference in units. For a surplus, enter the unit cost of the new batch. Missing units are deducted from the oldest batches first.</p></div></details><details class="faq-item"><summary class="faq-summary">Why won&#39;t the system allow deleting a product from the catalog?</summary><div class="faq-content"><p>Check the stock balance and production queue. Archiving hides the item from the active catalog while you sell the remaining stock. If a production order is open, complete or cancel it.</p></div></details><details class="faq-item"><summary class="faq-summary">How can I restore an accidentally archived item?</summary><div class="faq-content"><p>Select “Archived” and click the restore button on the product card. The item returns to the active catalog with its batches and prices.</p></div></details></div>',63))])}const w=o(d,[["render",u]]);export{b as __pageData,w as default};
